WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:04.780
Welcome to Ear Biscuits,

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:06.770
the podcast where two lifelong friends

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:09.360
talk about life for a long time.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.180
I'm Rhett.
And I'm Link.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:14.210
This week at the round
table of dim lighting,

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:18.400
we're talking about your legacy.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.790
What?

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:24.363
When you go to a funeral,
sometimes, as I did recently,

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:27.730
it puts these things on
the forefront of your mind,

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:31.620
thinking about like, man, when I pass,

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.980
how am I gonna be remembered?

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:38.980
And is there something
that I should be doing now

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:45.570
in order to facilitate
my memory, or my legacy?

00:00:46.245 --> 00:00:47.662
The first thing that comes to mind is

00:00:47.662 --> 00:00:50.150
you should be trying to
find a way to get that image

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:52.340
of you peeing chocolate into my mouth

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:54.080
off of the internet before you die.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.403
Yeah, and I do wanna talk about-

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.081
And I should actively also
be working towards that.

00:01:01.081 --> 00:01:02.320
That should be our only goal.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.200
Let's just stop this podcast right now.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:08.510
I do wanna talk about us
as a special case in this,

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:09.960
but you know what?

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.950
Maybe you're not an internet celebrity.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:13.870
Maybe you're just an avid listener

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:16.040
of two internet celebrities,

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:17.830
and that's okay.

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:20.170
You still need to tackle your legacy,

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:22.200
and how you wanna be remembered
is anything you can do.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.500
So I think we can put ourselves

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:27.023
in the shoes of the listener
as well and talk about that,

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:32.900
because yeah, I have been
in this mode of, okay,

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.550
passing away, remembering-

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:39.350
You were in the mode of passing away?

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.570
Please.
No, I'm not passing away.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:43.220
Technically, we all are slowly.

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:45.810
Yeah, I went to a funeral.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:48.360
I'm in the head space-
Good, good word.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.240
Of passing away and being remembered.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.220
So I just thought, let's have
a conversation about that.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:56.790
So I think the first half
is me sharing the experience

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:59.090
of everything surrounding this funeral.

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:01.310
And of course, I'm keeping it vague,

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:04.090
because once I start telling you about it,

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:05.680
I'll be telling you about it.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.260
And then I do think there's
a specific discovery

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:13.787
that then really got me thinking
about all of this stuff.

00:02:13.787 --> 00:02:16.880
Got you thinking?
More than just a funeral.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:17.713
Okay.

00:02:17.713 --> 00:02:19.800
More than just what other people say

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.030
about you after you die,

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.090
and a lot more about what you say

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:27.353
about yourself after you die.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.920
Okay, talking about holograms now.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:32.973
You're intrigued?

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:38.320
See, all this was teaser,
pretty lighthearted teaser.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.926
I guess it's gonna
start out kind of heavy.

00:02:40.926 --> 00:02:42.270
If you don't wanna hear

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:46.160
about passing of loved ones and funerals,

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.190
this is not the episode for you,

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:49.760
or role-playing your own death.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:52.013
That's not, I'm not saying
that the right way either.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:53.993
Anyway-
Proceed.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:59.203
All right, hard left
turn into being serious.

00:03:00.887 --> 00:03:04.450
As you know, I've talked
about it, I know you know,

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:07.130
but as I've talked about on Ear Biscuits,

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:09.730
my mom's husband Lewis

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:13.120
has not been good, good,
good for a long time.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.710
Of course, that was his catchphrase.

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:18.120
But then once he suffered
a number of strokes

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.080
about three years ago,

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:23.893
we never heard him say
good, good, good anymore.

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:30.330
And I believe after I went home in July,

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:34.798
I came back and talked
about it on an Ear Biscuit,

00:03:34.798 --> 00:03:37.780
my whole experience of going home.

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:38.740
If it wasn't that episode,

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:41.270
it was another one where
I was just really inspired

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:46.270
by my mom being so sacrificial
in her love for Lewis

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:53.420
to take care of him 24/7 as a nurse,

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:56.900
as well as a wife and companion for,

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:59.583
yeah, what went on for three years.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:03.480
He had a number of health
complications recently,

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:07.350
and we were tracking along with mom,

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:12.350
and it was a bit of a
surprise when he passed,

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.800
but he had taken a turn.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:21.830
So there was like, there were a few days,

00:04:21.830 --> 00:04:24.253
maybe a week leading up to it.

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:28.993
He had some complications from COVID.

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:33.200
He had a number of other things going on.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:37.413
But the long and short of
it is, he did pass away.

00:04:42.076 --> 00:04:45.050
I never called Lewis my stepdad,

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:48.220
because again, he got married to mom.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:49.707
You did call him your father-in-law.

00:04:49.707 --> 00:04:52.430
I called, I always called
him my father-in-law,

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:54.170
because I loved him so much.

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:55.790
I definitely loved him.

00:04:55.790 --> 00:04:56.752
And you know-

00:04:56.752 --> 00:04:59.530
You feel like stepdad
is not endearing enough.

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:01.800
Yeah, I'm not feeling that term.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:02.640
There should be a term

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:06.900
for when your stepdad steps
up and becomes your dad.

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:08.068
And I think you just call him dad.

00:05:08.068 --> 00:05:09.530
Well, he didn't become my dad either.

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:10.558
Okay.

00:05:10.558 --> 00:05:11.850
He just, he became-

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:13.458
'Cause you were an adult
already, when they got together.

00:05:13.458 --> 00:05:14.767
He became my mom's husband after-

00:05:14.767 --> 00:05:16.410
And that's how you're
always referred to him.

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:17.243
My mom's husband.

00:05:17.243 --> 00:05:19.330
And everybody'd be like, "Your stepdad?"

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:20.170
Right.

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:23.370
And that's not to say
that I didn't love him,

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:24.910
and still do love him.

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:29.773
And he was a Popeye to my kids,

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:35.163
as well as a wonderful,
wonderful husband to my mom.

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:37.980
They loved each other.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:41.180
The reason why, and you know what,

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:42.720
they gave people an opportunity

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.410
to stand up at the funeral

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:49.410
and say a little something about Lewis.

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:54.080
And I got up and I just-

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:56.040
And I have a firsthand account of this,

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:56.980
well, secondhand account,

00:05:56.980 --> 00:05:57.850
because my-
Yeah, you weren't there.

00:05:57.850 --> 00:05:58.850
My wife was there.

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:00.823
Yeah, that's right.

00:06:00.823 --> 00:06:01.656
Because my wife happened to be

00:06:01.656 --> 00:06:02.710
in North Carolina at the time.

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:03.590
Which was awesome.

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:04.580
Yeah, she was there.

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:05.630
I talked to her beforehand.

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:06.930
She said Link did great.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.470
Just in case you had
any doubts about that.

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:11.560
Well, I knew there was

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:14.475
gonna be like a popcorn sharing time.

00:06:14.475 --> 00:06:18.920
And I knew that I was
ready to go first, so that,

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:22.440
to prime the pump for other people to go.

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:26.970
Because they might not have
known and been prepared for it.

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:29.867
It's not walk up and go
behind the podium and talk-

00:06:29.867 --> 00:06:30.700
It's just stand up

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:32.100
at your spot-
Just stand up where you are,

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:37.100
because the family just felt
like that would give people

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:39.440
an opportunity to say
something from the heart,

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:41.320
but not put anybody on the spot

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:43.000
who really needed to prepare

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:44.530
a eulogy.
No microphones involved.

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:46.330
There was a microphone.
A passive mic.

00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:47.800
So I knew that this was gonna happen.

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:50.190
And so I was ready to
kind of prime the pump.

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:53.750
So I stood up first and
said my little piece,

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:55.020
and then other people said their thing,

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:57.521
but I did a very calculated,

00:06:57.521 --> 00:07:00.810
I'm not going to ramble on forever,

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:02.030
because then other people

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:03.630
will feel like they have to do that.

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:04.870
And a lot of me getting up,

00:07:04.870 --> 00:07:08.420
and the main reason I'm
getting up is obvious.

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:10.540
But a secondary reason I'm getting up is

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:13.770
to empower other people to
share what's on their heart,

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:16.790
by not being too long or too perfect

00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:18.060
or intimidating or anything.

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:20.633
I'm just gonna be myself
and keep it simple.

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:22.510
Okay.

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:23.630
But also keep it quick.

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:26.430
And that was the one thing
that I knew could be a problem.

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:27.720
How long did you go?

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:28.640
Longer than I wanted,

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:30.433
but I think it ended up being like,

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:36.940
it felt like four minutes, so
it was probably six minutes.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:38.577
Probably seven and a half.

00:07:38.577 --> 00:07:39.960
And it could have been seven and a half,

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:41.337
but like Jessie told you-

00:07:41.337 --> 00:07:42.937
You did great.
I did great.

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:47.280
But I was really glad to celebrate

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:50.430
he and mom's relationship,

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:52.260
and the things that I had already shared

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:54.510
with you and shared on this podcast

00:07:55.560 --> 00:08:00.560
that it really was a representation of,

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:03.560
a fulfillment of the
marriage vows they took,

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:07.426
in sickness and in health,
till death to us part.

00:08:07.426 --> 00:08:08.570
She was there with him.

00:08:08.570 --> 00:08:11.700
She went above and beyond, not as a wife,

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:15.367
but as a nurse, really, to care for him,

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:21.260
when she had lost so
much of him years ago.

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:26.260
I's been so difficult to see
both of them go through this,

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:27.933
but I say, you know what?

00:08:27.933 --> 00:08:32.933
She didn't do it as a
fulfillment of her vows.

00:08:33.110 --> 00:08:37.070
She did it because she loved him.

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:39.300
They had a great relationship.

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:41.850
They had a great life together.

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:44.320
They enjoyed each other.

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:49.320
And it's a beautiful thing that he was,

00:08:49.716 --> 00:08:51.700
the way he treated her

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:53.560
and the companionship
they gave each other,

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:57.355
he was worthy of the
care that she gave him.

00:08:57.355 --> 00:08:59.530
And that was the way that
I wanted to honor him

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:04.530
to his family and
friends there was to say,

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:08.620
I'm celebrating my mom for what she did

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:10.380
in service to him out of love,

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:12.940
not out of obligation.

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:17.560
And I'm so glad that she
had someone in her life

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:21.000
that was worthy of that
type of care and sacrifice,

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:25.160
because Lewis was an awesome guy.

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:27.030
He was loved by everybody
in the community.

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:29.430
That's why other people
wanted to say something.

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:35.370
The service was really beautiful,

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:38.010
and it was a celebration.

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:41.570
It made all of us feel very grateful

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:44.310
to have had him in our lives.

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:46.670
I will also say another note

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:49.150
that I got from Jessie about this funeral.

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:50.330
I didn't know if you were gonna talk

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:51.530
about this aspect of it.

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:58.040
We've been to many Christian,

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:01.840
maybe only Christian
funerals in our lives.

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:02.780
And I know that this
was a Christian funeral

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:05.313
and they went to a Methodist Church.

00:10:07.407 --> 00:10:10.940
In many of the funerals
that we went to growing up,

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:16.430
it was much less about the person

00:10:16.430 --> 00:10:18.510
and they made it about
the people in the audience

00:10:18.510 --> 00:10:21.320
and about them having to
make a decision about Jesus,

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:24.230
and it became this very
hardcore gospel presentation.

00:10:24.230 --> 00:10:25.063
And it was kind of like, yeah,

00:10:25.063 --> 00:10:27.280
he was a great dude, but what about you?

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:28.490
And where are you gonna spend eternity?

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:30.500
And we always felt like
it wasn't a real funeral

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:31.620
if the pastor didn't take

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:34.023
that hard turn into a gospel presentation.

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:40.260
And I was happy, the current
Rhett, not maybe the old Rhett,

00:10:40.420 --> 00:10:42.510
but the current Rhett
was very happy to hear

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:47.230
that while obviously it
was a Christian message,

00:10:47.230 --> 00:10:49.427
but it was really a
celebration of his life

00:10:49.427 --> 00:10:51.197
and remembering him.
It was,

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:57.073
I believe it was honoring to
God and honoring to his faith,

00:10:59.619 --> 00:11:01.330
and it was also honoring to his life.

00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:03.950
And it wasn't putting the congregation

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:06.040
on the spot in any way,

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:09.280
which can feel kind of cringey.

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:13.350
So yeah, it really was
a beautiful service.

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:15.383
Exploiting someone's death

00:11:15.383 --> 00:11:17.573
for the sake of an agenda.

00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:20.810
I'll get off my soapbox. But
I'm just happy to hear that-

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:22.680
Yeah, that didn't happen.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:27.020
Pastor did a great job, and
everybody else who talked,

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:28.660
including me, apparently.

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:30.290
Yeah, Link did great.

00:11:30.290 --> 00:11:31.680
The pastor did great.

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:32.840
Come to think of it,

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:36.963
she didn't say anybody
didn't do great, so-

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:39.683
Yeah, everybody did great.

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:45.920
That's not really what
it was about, but sure.

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:50.420
But the experience around it was one

00:11:50.420 --> 00:11:52.170
that I wouldn't trade for the world,

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:57.170
because Lewis passed away
on a Wednesday evening.

00:11:57.460 --> 00:12:01.490
And then I got on a plane the next morning

00:12:01.490 --> 00:12:03.360
to fly home to be with mom.

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:08.360
So within 22 hours of his
passing, I was there with mom.

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:11.533
So he died on a Wednesday night.

00:12:12.410 --> 00:12:15.750
And then I was able to get on a plane

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:17.320
the next morning and fly home,

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:21.920
so that by 22 hours later,
I was there with my mom.

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:26.920
And we actually had plans to, as a family,

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:28.683
we were coming in,

00:12:28.683 --> 00:12:30.230
we had already made plans a long time ago

00:12:30.230 --> 00:12:32.340
to come into town the following weekend

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:34.000
for Britton's wedding,

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:39.000
which was basically in the
next town over in Sanford.

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:42.750
So we were like, okay,

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:44.570
we all need to be here for the funeral.

00:12:44.570 --> 00:12:46.520
We may not be able to make the wedding.

00:12:47.570 --> 00:12:52.570
but it worked out with the family

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:54.240
and the funeral home and everything

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:57.870
to push the funeral to
the following Thursday.

00:12:57.870 --> 00:12:59.880
So he died on a Wednesday.
I arrived the next day.

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:03.650
One week later is when
we scheduled the funeral,

00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:08.250
so that my family could come
into town, go to the funeral,

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:10.080
and then stay through the wedding

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:12.500
as opposed to missing the wedding,

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:14.920
because we couldn't do
both if it was earlier,

00:13:14.920 --> 00:13:18.770
'cause they would be in jeopardy
of missing too much school.

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:21.170
So logistically, we worked that out,

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:23.350
but a byproduct of that,

00:13:23.350 --> 00:13:24.270
and that's just something

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:29.270
that I'll end up treasuring
forever I think is

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:34.323
it meant that I was with
my mom for six days,

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:39.883
all day, and then staying
at her house all night,

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:44.940
until Christy and the kids showed up.

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:46.270
And then we went to an Airbnb

00:13:46.270 --> 00:13:47.920
for the night before the funeral.

00:13:49.290 --> 00:13:50.650
And that turned out to be,

00:13:50.650 --> 00:13:52.870
it was great to be able
to be there for mom

00:13:52.870 --> 00:13:54.810
and for it not to be
something that was rushed.

00:13:54.810 --> 00:13:59.810
It's like when a loved one
dies across the country,

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:02.230
you go out there, but then you
gotta get back to your life,

00:14:02.230 --> 00:14:05.070
and you can't keep doing
a lot of your work,

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:07.880
and so you gotta go back,

00:14:07.880 --> 00:14:10.723
versus if you're in town,
you can show up and leave.

00:14:11.890 --> 00:14:13.590
With us being over here,
it's just one of those times

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:15.653
where it really hurts to not be there

00:14:15.653 --> 00:14:17.390
because you can't be there

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:19.660
for the people that you love the most.

00:14:19.660 --> 00:14:22.350
So it was nice that it worked out.

00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:26.013
And it was actually easier because it was,

00:14:26.890 --> 00:14:28.310
that same week is the week

00:14:28.310 --> 00:14:30.080
that you were on that trip with Locke.

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:31.920
So the same time that you were doing that.

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:34.470
That's why Jessie was there,
but you weren't there.

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:37.593
If anybody's wondering that.

00:14:38.769 --> 00:14:40.340
And it made it easier logistically.

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:42.733
Our shooting schedule was kind of cleared.

00:14:43.677 --> 00:14:45.000
And it ended up,

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:48.880
so I really felt released
from my work in a way

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:50.860
that I think I would've
made the right decision

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:54.210
to be with mom anyway,
maybe that whole time,

00:14:54.210 --> 00:14:58.200
but I just felt more, I didn't
have the pressure of like,

00:14:58.200 --> 00:15:02.090
okay, there's so much that
runs through the two of us.

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:05.310
I always get scared when it's
like, to do the right thing,

00:15:05.310 --> 00:15:08.340
it's gonna make things really
hard back here for work.

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:10.110
So it was nice to be released from that,

00:15:10.110 --> 00:15:13.533
because it just so happened you
were already gone that week.

00:15:15.650 --> 00:15:18.930
And I helped mom walk through
a lot of the logistics

00:15:18.930 --> 00:15:23.930
in working with Lewis's
family to like, I don't know,

00:15:23.997 --> 00:15:26.640
just to set things up, keep
things clean around the house.

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:30.150
My mom's an only child, I'm an only child.

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:34.928
So when it comes to her
in our side of the family,

00:15:34.928 --> 00:15:38.040
it's really just the two of us.

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:41.700
There's nobody else who's gonna
come in and help my mom out

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:46.193
except for a couple of her best friends

00:15:46.193 --> 00:15:48.500
who are gonna be in and out.

00:15:48.500 --> 00:15:51.253
So I did really need to step up.

00:15:52.820 --> 00:15:54.260
So I was able to do that.

00:15:54.260 --> 00:15:55.730
But every night,

00:15:55.730 --> 00:15:58.060
people were coming through
all throughout the day,

00:15:58.060 --> 00:16:00.840
but it was really every night,

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:02.820
people would stop showing
up when it got dark.

00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:05.170
And it would just be the
two of us hanging out,

00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:08.513
watching television, eating some more

00:16:08.513 --> 00:16:12.329
of that Bojangles that
everybody was dropping off.

00:16:12.329 --> 00:16:15.270
That's what people do, man.
Oh man, it was great.

00:16:15.270 --> 00:16:18.030
Bojangles has this
cinnamon twist thing now.

00:16:18.030 --> 00:16:20.670
It's like Bo-Berry
Biscuits are not as good

00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:21.797
as this cinnamon twist thing they got.

00:16:21.797 --> 00:16:22.740
Oh, okay, hold on.

00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:24.530
People were bringing
these cinnamon twists by.

00:16:24.530 --> 00:16:25.790
That might be sacrilegious.

00:16:25.790 --> 00:16:27.950
I'm just planting a seed, man.

00:16:27.950 --> 00:16:29.130
Nothing with cinnamon in it is

00:16:29.130 --> 00:16:30.270
better than a Bo-Berry Biscuit.

00:16:30.270 --> 00:16:33.310
It's just, oh, it's so good.

00:16:33.310 --> 00:16:35.103
Okay.
But I had both.

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:38.610
So we'd be eating some of that.

00:16:38.610 --> 00:16:40.680
Of course, I did go to the grocery store

00:16:40.680 --> 00:16:43.433
and buy my supplies, which were-

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:49.243
Water, ChapStick.
Coffee and cereal.

00:16:50.980 --> 00:16:52.630
So I'd get me a bowl of cereal,

00:16:52.630 --> 00:16:54.592
and I'd sit down there with my mom.

00:16:54.592 --> 00:16:56.150
Your mom's not a coffee drinker?

00:16:56.150 --> 00:16:58.200
No, so I got to make sure I got my coffee.

00:16:58.200 --> 00:17:00.130
Wow, she's never been a coffee drinker.

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:01.170
Nope.

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:05.530
And then we would just sit
down and talk every night.

00:17:05.530 --> 00:17:08.393
Of course, we would talk,
she'd be processing,

00:17:10.280 --> 00:17:12.810
this slowly becoming real.

00:17:12.810 --> 00:17:14.100
You're in shock.

00:17:14.100 --> 00:17:18.120
It's like, you might think it
was expected, but it wasn't,

00:17:18.120 --> 00:17:23.120
and you really can't fully
expect to lose your mate.

00:17:24.760 --> 00:17:27.910
So you can't be fully prepared for that.

00:17:27.910 --> 00:17:31.690
So whenever she wanted
to talk about Lewis,

00:17:31.690 --> 00:17:36.690
and memories or what they
had just been going through.

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:40.540
I was just able to listen.

00:17:40.540 --> 00:17:43.750
But then we would also
talk about other things.

00:17:43.750 --> 00:17:47.960
I brought up stuff like
memories from my childhood

00:17:47.960 --> 00:17:50.600
that I was talking about in therapy.

00:17:50.600 --> 00:17:52.880
I would bring up, I was like,
"Mom, I remember this thing."

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:53.870
I'm not gonna share it here.

00:17:53.870 --> 00:17:58.803
But yeah, I kind of got
her perspective on it.

00:18:03.090 --> 00:18:04.410
I don't care, I'll share it.

00:18:04.410 --> 00:18:07.133
I remember when I was a kid, I would,

00:18:08.410 --> 00:18:12.130
one of my first memories
was at First Baptist Church,

00:18:12.130 --> 00:18:15.430
I got, it was me and Jimmy, my stepdad.

00:18:15.430 --> 00:18:16.997
He was legitimately

00:18:16.997 --> 00:18:17.840
my stepdad.
Your father-in-law.

00:18:17.840 --> 00:18:18.990
Now my father-in-law.

00:18:19.830 --> 00:18:21.190
Your first father-in-law.

00:18:21.190 --> 00:18:22.760
I remember in the middle of the service,

00:18:22.760 --> 00:18:23.610
I was doing something.

00:18:23.610 --> 00:18:25.850
He took me out of the service

00:18:25.850 --> 00:18:27.220
and walked down the center aisle

00:18:27.220 --> 00:18:30.020
and took me out and gave me a lecture,

00:18:30.020 --> 00:18:31.660
and then brought me back in

00:18:31.660 --> 00:18:34.610
to apologize to my mom during the service.

00:18:34.610 --> 00:18:36.780
And I don't even remember
what I was doing,

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:39.150
but I remember just kind
of being traumatized

00:18:39.150 --> 00:18:42.875
by the fact that I was paraded
out in the middle of church,

00:18:42.875 --> 00:18:45.677
and back in while everybody
was looking at me.

00:18:45.677 --> 00:18:47.690
And it was like-
He didn't whoop you though.

00:18:47.690 --> 00:18:48.740
He didn't whoop me.

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:51.447
And mom reminded me.

00:18:51.447 --> 00:18:52.490
"Well, when you came back in,

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:55.390
you had a flower in your hand.

00:18:55.390 --> 00:18:58.930
You'd picked a flower and
you came and gave it to me."

00:18:58.930 --> 00:19:01.400
And I was like four years old.

00:19:01.400 --> 00:19:02.713
Five years old at most.

00:19:03.880 --> 00:19:04.883
And she was reminding me of that.

00:19:04.883 --> 00:19:07.000
Pick a flower for your mama, boy.

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:08.340
Yeah, and she was like-

00:19:08.340 --> 00:19:09.410
I don't care, just get one.

00:19:09.410 --> 00:19:11.700
She gave me some insight
into what was going on there

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:13.970
that we had never talked about.

00:19:13.970 --> 00:19:17.370
We had this very adult conversation about,

00:19:17.370 --> 00:19:21.103
what was it, so many
things that, experiences,

00:19:22.620 --> 00:19:24.120
these little bits and memories

00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:25.853
that you have when you're a child,

00:19:26.780 --> 00:19:29.760
that I never sat down
and picked my mom's brain

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:34.520
on what was happening
that, what did I do wrong?

00:19:34.520 --> 00:19:35.797
Did she remember?

00:19:35.797 --> 00:19:37.790
She said I couldn't sit still.

00:19:37.790 --> 00:19:40.640
And she thought that the
punishment didn't fit the crime.

00:19:41.930 --> 00:19:44.653
So there was a bit of vindication there,

00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:49.380
but that's just the nature
of the type of conversations

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:52.300
that we ended up having to
the point where I was like,

00:19:52.300 --> 00:19:53.810
maybe it was the first
night or the second night,

00:19:53.810 --> 00:19:55.157
I just turned to mom and I was like,

00:19:55.157 --> 00:19:59.540
"You know, this is the first time

00:19:59.540 --> 00:20:01.790
that we've ever spent
this much time together

00:20:01.790 --> 00:20:04.203
just the two of us since,

00:20:05.160 --> 00:20:07.330
definitely before I got married."

00:20:07.330 --> 00:20:09.340
Once I got married, I'm
always bringing Christy.

00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:10.483
It's like, hey, we're,

00:20:11.540 --> 00:20:12.890
I'm not coming and spending the night

00:20:12.890 --> 00:20:15.425
with my mom once I'm a married man.

00:20:15.425 --> 00:20:16.510
It just doesn't happen.

00:20:16.510 --> 00:20:19.100
Well, in some relationships, it happens.

00:20:19.100 --> 00:20:21.050
Yeah, but-
Not in yours though.

00:20:21.050 --> 00:20:23.900
Yeah, I'm grateful for that.

00:20:23.900 --> 00:20:27.060
And it's like, and we never,

00:20:27.060 --> 00:20:28.840
and back when we did live together,

00:20:28.840 --> 00:20:32.633
we were never having adult conversations.

00:20:35.242 --> 00:20:39.310
So it was nice to be there for her,

00:20:39.310 --> 00:20:41.700
but also to kind of have this,

00:20:41.700 --> 00:20:46.620
to develop this new rapport
between the two of us.

00:20:46.620 --> 00:20:49.940
Another time, I just
kind of got in my mind

00:20:49.940 --> 00:20:54.040
questions I would ask her,
'cause it's, and I will clarify.

00:20:54.040 --> 00:20:58.210
It's not that we weren't
close, but growing up,

00:20:58.210 --> 00:20:59.293
it was just that-

00:21:01.210 --> 00:21:02.680
Most people don't talk to their parents

00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:04.143
about these things.
Yeah, she loved,

00:21:05.030 --> 00:21:06.670
my mom was the sweetest mom.

00:21:06.670 --> 00:21:08.450
I can't come up with any trash

00:21:08.450 --> 00:21:09.930
to talk about her to my therapist.

00:21:09.930 --> 00:21:11.330
I'll put it to you that way.

00:21:12.460 --> 00:21:15.870
She never actively did
anything to me that I could,

00:21:15.870 --> 00:21:18.760
that I need to pay for therapy for.

00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:20.483
I could say that honestly.

00:21:22.070 --> 00:21:25.650
But yeah, it's not like in college,

00:21:25.650 --> 00:21:28.660
I called her and talked to
her on the phone all the time,

00:21:28.660 --> 00:21:31.520
we had this dynamic relationship.

00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:33.973
But we've always had a good relationship.

00:21:35.480 --> 00:21:37.150
But this was kind of
like a different level

00:21:37.150 --> 00:21:39.100
and a different facet to it,

00:21:39.100 --> 00:21:43.240
which was surprising.

00:21:43.240 --> 00:21:46.367
I started coming up with
questions to ask her like,

00:21:46.367 --> 00:21:50.910
"Mom, what do you think
about me being famous?"

00:21:50.910 --> 00:21:55.267
Just to get a kick out of
how she, and she was like,

00:21:55.267 --> 00:21:57.323
"Why are you asking me that?"

00:21:57.323 --> 00:21:59.857
She's getting paranoid, like,

00:21:59.857 --> 00:22:01.640
"Do you think I'm not proud of you?"

00:22:01.640 --> 00:22:03.260
I'm like, "No, no, no, I
know you're proud of me.

00:22:03.260 --> 00:22:06.100
It's just like, do you-

00:22:06.100 --> 00:22:08.800
Is it weird?
Do you, is it weird?

00:22:08.800 --> 00:22:12.275
Do you actually know
that I'm, she was like,

00:22:12.275 --> 00:22:14.580
"Well, I know that you're famous.

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:16.290
I do know that.

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:17.760
I go to the grocery store,

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:20.480
and people always have

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:22.380
something to say about you and Rhett."

00:22:23.670 --> 00:22:26.435
And I'm like, okay, so
you don't really know.

00:22:26.435 --> 00:22:30.450
I gotta take my mom
somewhere to prove my fame.

00:22:30.450 --> 00:22:31.550
I don't know what it is.

00:22:31.550 --> 00:22:33.030
Like when dad came out there that time

00:22:33.030 --> 00:22:35.683
when we had the buddy system billboard,

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:38.367
I took him out on a Hollywood
Boulevard and was like,

00:22:38.367 --> 00:22:39.780
"Look up there, see that billboard?

00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:41.629
That's me in Hollywood.

00:22:41.629 --> 00:22:44.210
That kind of did it for my dad.

00:22:44.210 --> 00:22:46.433
Of course, he definitely knows I'm famous.

00:22:49.230 --> 00:22:50.770
But that's the type of conversations

00:22:50.770 --> 00:22:53.270
that mom and I were having.

00:22:53.270 --> 00:22:58.270
And at some point in the
conversation, I remembered,

00:22:58.710 --> 00:23:02.500
as we were talking about Lewis,
I was like, "You know what?

00:23:02.500 --> 00:23:06.950
Lewis mentioned to me,
mom, do you know this?

00:23:06.950 --> 00:23:08.330
I think he said it in front of you too

00:23:08.330 --> 00:23:13.330
that he was writing down his life story."

00:23:13.550 --> 00:23:15.588
Or I don't know how he put it.

00:23:15.588 --> 00:23:17.430
I don't know if he said I'm
writing down my life story,

00:23:17.430 --> 00:23:22.380
but in my mind it was like,
oh, Lewis is writing a memoir.

00:23:22.380 --> 00:23:24.070
It's kind of how I processed it.

00:23:24.070 --> 00:23:26.850
And she was like, "Yes.

00:23:26.850 --> 00:23:29.290
I would give him such a
hard time about that."

00:23:29.290 --> 00:23:30.123
Oh really?

00:23:30.123 --> 00:23:33.400
He said, "I'm gonna write a book."

00:23:33.400 --> 00:23:35.703
So many things have happened
to me, I'm gonna write a book.

00:23:35.703 --> 00:23:39.610
And mom said, "And now I just kind of,

00:23:39.610 --> 00:23:40.900
I feel kind of bad for him,

00:23:40.900 --> 00:23:42.730
'cause I just gave him
a hard time about it.

00:23:42.730 --> 00:23:44.673
I was like, "So you never read it?"

00:23:44.673 --> 00:23:45.977
She was like, "No."

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:48.183
And it was like,

00:23:48.183 --> 00:23:52.140
but the way that Lewis talked
about what he wrote was,

00:23:52.140 --> 00:23:54.667
or that what he was writing was,

00:23:54.667 --> 00:23:57.210
"I'm just writing down some stories from,

00:23:57.210 --> 00:23:58.810
I got these stories,
things that happened to me.

00:23:58.810 --> 00:24:00.680
I'm writing them down."

00:24:00.680 --> 00:24:03.560
And so he would downplay.

00:24:03.560 --> 00:24:07.020
He didn't say, "I'm writing
and gonna publish a book."

00:24:07.020 --> 00:24:08.680
And I know I sound like Bill Clinton, but-

00:24:08.680 --> 00:24:10.020
Well, so does he-
There's a little bit of-

00:24:10.020 --> 00:24:11.570
Yeah, you sound like Lewis to me.

00:24:11.570 --> 00:24:13.013
Okay, good.

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:20.127
So I went on his computer and-

00:24:20.127 --> 00:24:21.893
I guess it was not password-protected.

00:24:23.400 --> 00:24:24.840
No.

00:24:24.840 --> 00:24:25.850
And I just started-

00:24:25.850 --> 00:24:26.880
Well, if you're gonna write a memoir

00:24:26.880 --> 00:24:27.730
and not keep it in the cloud,

00:24:27.730 --> 00:24:29.610
then don't put a password
on your computer.

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:31.920
I don't think he has a cloud.

00:24:31.920 --> 00:24:32.753
Exactly.

00:24:33.880 --> 00:24:35.770
The cloud is the computer.

00:24:35.770 --> 00:24:37.470
Yeah, Lewis didn't have a cloud.

00:24:39.520 --> 00:24:41.470
I remember at the time,

00:24:41.470 --> 00:24:43.490
and this was maybe a couple of years ago

00:24:43.490 --> 00:24:48.490
that he mentioned this, that
I was thinking, wow, this,

00:24:50.970 --> 00:24:52.920
if he's really following through with this

00:24:52.920 --> 00:24:54.300
and he seems serious about it,

00:24:54.300 --> 00:24:58.515
this is something that
one day when he does pass,

00:24:58.515 --> 00:25:01.750
this will be a nice thing to have.

00:25:01.750 --> 00:25:04.830
Maybe he's writing it for his
kids or, I haven't read it,

00:25:04.830 --> 00:25:06.220
and he didn't say who
he was writing it for,

00:25:06.220 --> 00:25:10.090
but that thought entered my
mind and kind of stuck in there.

00:25:10.090 --> 00:25:12.270
And then it popped back in.

00:25:12.270 --> 00:25:15.310
I was like, oh man, yeah, this is,

00:25:15.310 --> 00:25:16.913
he's been working on this thing.

00:25:17.830 --> 00:25:19.300
I know not in the past three years,

00:25:19.300 --> 00:25:21.660
but before that, he would
say he was working on it.

00:25:21.660 --> 00:25:26.660
So I went on his computer, and
I'll tell you what I found,

00:25:27.040 --> 00:25:28.520
but first let's do an ad.

00:25:28.520 --> 00:25:29.994
Doesn't this feel kind of cheap?

00:25:29.994 --> 00:25:30.827
That's good.
You're gonna throw

00:25:30.827 --> 00:25:32.214
the ads in-
I like that.

00:25:32.214 --> 00:25:33.500
You might as well just throw them in

00:25:33.500 --> 00:25:35.453
at a good cliffhanger.
I agree.

00:25:36.890 --> 00:25:39.440
We want you to check out Stevie's podcast.

00:25:39.440 --> 00:25:40.730
Best Friends-
If you have not done it.

00:25:40.730 --> 00:25:42.560
Best Friends Back, Alright!

00:25:42.560 --> 00:25:45.650
The podcast that she has with
her high school best friend,

00:25:45.650 --> 00:25:47.410
they're reconnecting via podcast

00:25:47.410 --> 00:25:49.920
in the episode that just came out is

00:25:49.920 --> 00:25:54.310
all about how Stevie went from Stephanie,

00:25:54.310 --> 00:25:59.310
yeah, that's the name
on her ID, to Stevie.

00:26:01.270 --> 00:26:05.030
So there was a point in her high school

00:26:05.030 --> 00:26:07.240
where she went from Stephanie to Stevie

00:26:07.240 --> 00:26:08.527
and she talks about that.

