Well, maybe you just
needed to get a divorce.

00:39:17.560 --> 00:39:19.010
Let's read between the lines here.

00:39:19.010 --> 00:39:20.320
Baby, I love you,

00:39:20.320 --> 00:39:23.163
but we're getting divorced
because I'm bothered by pollen.

00:39:24.570 --> 00:39:25.460
That's the only reason.

00:39:25.460 --> 00:39:27.000
We can stay together.

00:39:27.000 --> 00:39:28.390
We just need to legally divorce,

00:39:28.390 --> 00:39:29.677
'cause that might be the key.

00:39:29.677 --> 00:39:34.677
"1982, taught math in
prison for 12 years."

00:39:35.990 --> 00:39:37.210
What?

00:39:37.210 --> 00:39:40.420
He took a job through Central
Carolina Community College.

00:39:40.420 --> 00:39:42.173
I know you love CCCC.

00:39:43.900 --> 00:39:45.870
They ask him to substitute teach

00:39:45.870 --> 00:39:50.653
at the Harnett County Correctional
Center, aka the prison.

00:39:51.530 --> 00:39:55.660
And so he said, "I started,
and I quite liked it.

00:39:55.660 --> 00:39:58.427
So I just kept teaching
there for 12 years."

00:39:59.890 --> 00:40:01.023
He writes,

00:40:02.927 --> 00:40:06.590
"What's the difference between
an inmate and an outmate?

00:40:06.590 --> 00:40:10.120
The outmate is a person that
has not been caught yet."

00:40:10.120 --> 00:40:11.377
Wow.

00:40:11.377 --> 00:40:15.100
"The students have sports just
like we do out of prison."

00:40:15.100 --> 00:40:18.610
'Cause he taught younger kids,

00:40:18.610 --> 00:40:21.093
and then he taught adults in the prison.

00:40:21.993 --> 00:40:23.980
So when he talks about the students,

00:40:23.980 --> 00:40:24.987
well, he is talking about all prisoners.

00:40:24.987 --> 00:40:25.940
"The students have sports,

00:40:25.940 --> 00:40:27.470
just like we do out of prison.

00:40:27.470 --> 00:40:29.760
I had a student that was
the weightlifting champion

00:40:29.760 --> 00:40:32.273
for all of Eastern region of the US.

00:40:33.200 --> 00:40:35.070
A prison weightlifting champion?

00:40:35.070 --> 00:40:35.903
He was, no,

00:40:35.903 --> 00:40:38.620
he was weightlifting champion
before he was in prison.

00:40:38.620 --> 00:40:40.747
So this was a huge guy.

00:40:40.747 --> 00:40:42.730
"I had him for two semesters.

00:40:42.730 --> 00:40:46.090
After the two semesters,
he was back on the yard.

00:40:46.090 --> 00:40:48.320
Every morning, as I came
across the prison yard,

00:40:48.320 --> 00:40:51.630
he would meet me and ask,
'How's everything going?'

00:40:51.630 --> 00:40:53.470
Some mornings, he would ask me

00:40:53.470 --> 00:40:55.443
if anyone was giving me any trouble.

00:40:56.490 --> 00:40:58.760
After a year or so, I
asked him one morning,

00:40:58.760 --> 00:41:03.527
'Why do you ask me that
question about every morning?'

00:41:04.610 --> 00:41:06.070
He quickly answered me.

00:41:06.070 --> 00:41:07.443
'Mr. Cummings, I don't want anyone

00:41:07.443 --> 00:41:09.810
to give you any trouble
or a difficult time.

00:41:09.810 --> 00:41:12.347
You were fair to all
of us while in school,

00:41:12.347 --> 00:41:14.070
and you deserve the same.

00:41:14.070 --> 00:41:16.690
If anyone gives you any
trouble, you tell me,

00:41:16.690 --> 00:41:19.950
and I will take care of them at night

00:41:19.950 --> 00:41:21.343
when they are not in school.

00:41:23.860 --> 00:41:26.310
They won't bother you anymore.'"

00:41:26.310 --> 00:41:27.143
Wow.

00:41:27.143 --> 00:41:28.197
Sounds like he's saying,

00:41:28.197 --> 00:41:30.469
"Hey, if somebody's bothering
you, I'll kill them."

00:41:30.469 --> 00:41:33.930
Yeah, that's what I'm
understanding from it.

00:41:33.930 --> 00:41:37.780
He says, "I was looked after
in ways I didn't even know.

00:41:37.780 --> 00:41:39.540
He never told me until I asked

00:41:39.540 --> 00:41:42.380
why he asked me almost every morning

00:41:42.380 --> 00:41:44.107
is anyone causing any trouble."

00:41:45.338 --> 00:41:47.328
The way he'd write down his story-

00:41:47.328 --> 00:41:48.161
Just to tie a bow on it.

00:41:48.161 --> 00:41:49.503
Just to tie a bow on it.

00:41:49.503 --> 00:41:51.670
Just in case you lost track.

00:41:51.670 --> 00:41:55.120
He didn't let, it wasn't like I'm,

00:41:55.120 --> 00:41:57.103
he had these experiences.

00:41:58.080 --> 00:42:00.080
The way that he tells
them is so approachable,

00:42:00.080 --> 00:42:01.700
it's so true to who he was.

00:42:01.700 --> 00:42:05.470
I can hear him saying these things.

00:42:05.470 --> 00:42:09.280
Like I said, these stories,
some of them, he would tell us,

00:42:09.280 --> 00:42:11.920
and he wrote them down
how he would tell them.

00:42:11.920 --> 00:42:16.920
It wasn't like, I gotta make
this into more than it is.

00:42:18.020 --> 00:42:21.403
I've gotta make this memoir-able.

00:42:22.290 --> 00:42:24.600
It wasn't about the writing of it.

00:42:24.600 --> 00:42:27.060
It was about the experience of it.

00:42:27.060 --> 00:42:31.040
And I love the fact that
nothing got in the way of him

00:42:31.040 --> 00:42:35.070
sitting down at his computer
and writing this stuff down.

00:42:35.070 --> 00:42:38.850
And it's such a treasure.

00:42:38.850 --> 00:42:40.487
He kept going.

00:42:40.487 --> 00:42:42.960
"And yes, working with
prisons was different,

00:42:42.960 --> 00:42:45.240
but it's that way in real life.

00:42:45.240 --> 00:42:46.860
People are all very different.

00:42:46.860 --> 00:42:48.813
Some inmates and some out.

00:42:49.890 --> 00:42:50.910
You have to learn to work

00:42:50.910 --> 00:42:52.860
with all kinds of people in real life.

00:42:52.860 --> 00:42:54.927
It may be in school, job, church,

00:42:54.927 --> 00:42:58.920
community work, recreation,
factory, et cetera.

00:42:58.920 --> 00:43:00.270
And I had the opportunity to work

00:43:00.270 --> 00:43:02.930
with private school
kids, public school kids,

00:43:02.930 --> 00:43:06.523
parents, coaches,
administration, and prisoners.

00:43:07.560 --> 00:43:09.550
Youth prisons for three years,

00:43:09.550 --> 00:43:12.120
and adult prisons for nine years.

00:43:12.120 --> 00:43:15.640
Lots of great experiences and
worked with great friends,

00:43:15.640 --> 00:43:17.277
staff and students."

00:43:18.260 --> 00:43:22.150
It's like he had wisdom to share,

00:43:22.150 --> 00:43:24.160
and it didn't have to be,

00:43:24.160 --> 00:43:26.103
yeah, it didn't have to be souped up.

00:43:27.162 --> 00:43:30.380
I just love the fact that it captured

00:43:30.380 --> 00:43:34.710
this down to earth wisdom that he had.

00:43:34.710 --> 00:43:38.650
And the fact that he was so goodhearted.

00:43:38.650 --> 00:43:42.170
Every time that we,
every time you saw him,

00:43:42.170 --> 00:43:45.210
he had a story to tell, he
had a smile on his face.

00:43:45.210 --> 00:43:48.263
He was a special guy.

00:43:48.263 --> 00:43:51.300
And he and mom were such a pair.

00:43:51.300 --> 00:43:52.870
That's the thing that mom and I were

00:43:52.870 --> 00:43:55.210
able to talk about and remembering,

00:43:55.210 --> 00:43:58.853
'cause what I didn't tell
you was, my mom said,

00:44:00.327 --> 00:44:01.790
"Can you print that off for me?"

00:44:01.790 --> 00:44:03.500
And I'm like, "Well, your
printer doesn't work.

00:44:03.500 --> 00:44:07.840
And I'm not about to start
becoming your IT guy.

00:44:07.840 --> 00:44:10.520
That's not the main thing I'm here for.

00:44:10.520 --> 00:44:14.060
But what I will do, mom,
is I'll read it to you."

00:44:14.060 --> 00:44:17.100
So I started reading
it, and we would start,

00:44:17.100 --> 00:44:18.350
we read through the whole thing.

00:44:18.350 --> 00:44:19.850
I read it out loud to her.

00:44:19.850 --> 00:44:22.118
She was like, "This is
kinda like a bedtime story."

00:44:23.640 --> 00:44:27.960
And we got to talking about
everything that I was reading,

00:44:27.960 --> 00:44:30.720
that we couldn't get through
all of it in one night.

00:44:30.720 --> 00:44:32.970
And I had to stop, and I was
like, "Mom, it's getting late.

00:44:32.970 --> 00:44:34.100
You're gonna have to go to bed.

00:44:34.100 --> 00:44:35.247
And she was like, "No, no."

00:44:35.247 --> 00:44:37.604
It's just like putting a kid to bed.

00:44:37.604 --> 00:44:39.460
And so then the next night, she was like,

00:44:39.460 --> 00:44:42.897
it got to be like 7:30, and she was like,

00:44:42.897 --> 00:44:46.000
"You gonna read some more to me.

00:44:46.000 --> 00:44:48.070
We gotta pick up where
you left off last night."

00:44:48.070 --> 00:44:50.160
And I was like, "Well, I
gotta charge my phone first,

00:44:50.160 --> 00:44:52.027
'cause we don't want it to
die in the middle of it."

00:44:52.027 --> 00:44:54.210
And she had to wait 30 minutes.

00:44:54.210 --> 00:44:55.210
She was so excited.

00:44:55.210 --> 00:44:57.713
Couldn't plug it in.
Couldn't plug it in.

00:44:59.079 --> 00:45:00.710
I kind of wanted to mess with her.

00:45:00.710 --> 00:45:03.240
I was like, "We gotta wait
until it charges, mom."

00:45:03.240 --> 00:45:07.010
But yeah, it just struck me,

00:45:07.010 --> 00:45:09.700
when we went back through his memories,

00:45:09.700 --> 00:45:14.700
even though she got one
line in the whole thing,

00:45:15.090 --> 00:45:18.303
and it was like the year
of their second marriage,

00:45:19.380 --> 00:45:21.033
married for the second time.

00:45:26.071 --> 00:45:26.904
Oh wow, man.

00:45:26.904 --> 00:45:27.737
So I gotta to that point in the thing

00:45:27.737 --> 00:45:28.750
and I read it to her.
Hold on, hold on.

00:45:28.750 --> 00:45:30.070
Let me just say,

00:45:30.070 --> 00:45:32.960
as the guy who was not willing
to print it out for her,

00:45:32.960 --> 00:45:33.850
but willing to read it,

00:45:33.850 --> 00:45:35.450
you could have embellished that.

00:45:36.670 --> 00:45:37.990
Got married for the second time

00:45:37.990 --> 00:45:39.750
to the love of my life.
I've thought about it.

00:45:39.750 --> 00:45:44.620
But by this point in the reading of it,

00:45:44.620 --> 00:45:45.947
I wanted to be true to it.

00:45:45.947 --> 00:45:48.580
And plus, we both had an appreciation.

00:45:48.580 --> 00:45:50.880
Mom busted out laughing.

00:45:50.880 --> 00:45:52.350
She was like, "Now I don't feel so bad

00:45:52.350 --> 00:45:54.850
about giving him such a
hard time writing a book."

00:45:55.840 --> 00:45:57.720
Well, I think it's because he was

00:45:57.720 --> 00:46:01.670
actively experiencing a
relationship with her,

00:46:01.670 --> 00:46:04.300
and the stuff that he was writing down was

00:46:04.300 --> 00:46:07.550
a little bit more about
things he was remembering.

00:46:07.550 --> 00:46:09.730
And so much of it was through the lens

00:46:09.730 --> 00:46:12.660
of teaching and coaching,

00:46:12.660 --> 00:46:15.790
and it was that world, like 90%.

00:46:15.790 --> 00:46:17.817
If it wasn't about pollen,

00:46:17.817 --> 00:46:20.960
he wrote one line about
each kid being born too,

00:46:20.960 --> 00:46:21.793
I will say that.
I think

00:46:21.793 --> 00:46:23.850
Bothered by Pollen is
probably a better name

00:46:23.850 --> 00:46:25.536
for the book than Time Line.

00:46:25.536 --> 00:46:26.980
Right.

00:46:26.980 --> 00:46:30.690
Sounds like a Robert
Frost thing or something.

00:46:30.690 --> 00:46:34.010
Part of me feels, he wrote
so many other things,

00:46:34.010 --> 00:46:36.730
but mom and I just got an appreciation

00:46:36.730 --> 00:46:39.020
for how perfect they were for each other.

00:46:39.020 --> 00:46:44.020
And how those 15-plus years,

00:46:44.060 --> 00:46:46.430
I don't know, how many years
did they have together?

00:46:46.430 --> 00:46:47.263
I can't remember.

00:46:47.263 --> 00:46:48.220
Well, you just said right there.

00:46:48.220 --> 00:46:50.800
What year did he say
married for the second time?

00:46:50.800 --> 00:46:51.940
I didn't paste that into this.

00:46:51.940 --> 00:46:54.090
I'm not looking at the entire document.

00:46:54.090 --> 00:46:57.220
In 2002, he talked about a
near death experience he had

00:46:57.220 --> 00:47:00.560
where he almost got crushed
by a dump truck because he,

00:47:00.560 --> 00:47:03.040
he had a dump truck on his farm,

00:47:03.040 --> 00:47:05.467
and he reached, it was up.
Oh God.

00:47:05.467 --> 00:47:08.180
And it was a tank full of water.

00:47:08.180 --> 00:47:12.290
And then he reached underneath
it to pull out something.

00:47:12.290 --> 00:47:13.860
He was like a dummy.

00:47:13.860 --> 00:47:17.040
I pulled this thing that I
thought was a loose wire.

00:47:17.040 --> 00:47:20.243
And it tripped the whole thing,
and it's the whole, like-

00:47:21.390 --> 00:47:22.223
The hydraulic.

00:47:22.223 --> 00:47:25.400
Huge truck, just like the
whole dump truck slammed down.

00:47:25.400 --> 00:47:28.340
And the next thing he knew,
he was standing beside it,

00:47:28.340 --> 00:47:31.140
and he did not know what happened.

00:47:31.140 --> 00:47:33.890
He just had a few scrapes on his chest,

00:47:33.890 --> 00:47:35.333
but he wasn't hurt.

00:47:37.110 --> 00:47:40.427
And somebody walked by and they said,

00:47:40.427 --> 00:47:45.320
"What, Lewis, what happened to you?"

00:47:45.320 --> 00:47:47.950
And he was white as a sheet.

00:47:47.950 --> 00:47:50.377
And Lewis said, "Nothing."

00:47:51.600 --> 00:47:53.360
And then the guy said, "Well,
you're white is a sheet."

00:47:53.360 --> 00:47:56.210
And he said, "Well, I
almost got crushed by this.

00:47:56.210 --> 00:47:57.050
And I don't know what happened."

00:47:57.050 --> 00:48:00.030
But his original answer
was nothing happened,

00:48:00.030 --> 00:48:02.963
because I guess technically
nothing happened.

00:48:04.120 --> 00:48:06.650
But he talks about that
near death experience, and-

00:48:06.650 --> 00:48:07.730
That doesn't coincide

00:48:07.730 --> 00:48:09.360
when he stopped being bothered by pollen.

00:48:09.360 --> 00:48:10.193
Nope.

00:48:10.193 --> 00:48:11.290
It's like, that was 10 years later.

00:48:11.290 --> 00:48:13.190
Yeah, and then in 2010,

00:48:13.190 --> 00:48:16.650
he talked about how he was
diagnosed with polycythemia vera,

00:48:16.650 --> 00:48:19.820
which is like, your body
makes too much blood.

00:48:19.820 --> 00:48:22.610
So he had to go to the doctor and just-

00:48:22.610 --> 00:48:23.443
Leak?

00:48:23.443 --> 00:48:26.980
Leak a pin of blood every month or so.

00:48:26.980 --> 00:48:29.550
But he said the way that he found out was,

00:48:29.550 --> 00:48:30.767
they were doing a bunch of tests

00:48:30.767 --> 00:48:32.260
and they started taking blood,

00:48:32.260 --> 00:48:34.710
and he said as the blood
came out of the needle,

00:48:34.710 --> 00:48:37.810
it fell in the bag like ketchup
falling out of a bottle.

00:48:37.810 --> 00:48:39.150
Oh my gosh.

00:48:39.150 --> 00:48:40.980
It doesn't come out unless you squeeze it.

00:48:40.980 --> 00:48:43.070
Oh, oh, oh, oh oh.

00:48:43.070 --> 00:48:44.880
How did you read that
without freaking out?

00:48:44.880 --> 00:48:47.170
I've freaked out about it a bunch,

00:48:47.170 --> 00:48:48.003
but I've read this a bunch now.

00:48:48.003 --> 00:48:49.930
So it's like thick blood?

00:48:49.930 --> 00:48:51.460
Thick blood.
Oh gosh.

00:48:51.460 --> 00:48:53.660
That's when he decided to retire.

00:48:53.660 --> 00:48:58.660
And he moved into he and mom's
phase of happiness together.

00:48:59.040 --> 00:49:01.330
But he had given up
coaching for the most part,

00:49:01.330 --> 00:49:05.050
and he signs the whole
thing happy, happy, happy.

00:49:05.050 --> 00:49:06.610
Thank you, Lord.

00:49:06.610 --> 00:49:09.240
Happy, happy, happy was the
Duck Dynasty catchphrase

00:49:09.240 --> 00:49:10.179
that he modified into good,

00:49:10.179 --> 00:49:12.860
good, good.
Good, good, good, yeah.

00:49:12.860 --> 00:49:17.220
And there's so much more
there that is not my business

00:49:17.220 --> 00:49:19.380
reading anymore of it publicly,

00:49:19.380 --> 00:49:22.410
but I just wanted to do that
to honor him as a person.

00:49:22.410 --> 00:49:25.963
But you can see how this got me thinking.

00:49:27.910 --> 00:49:30.041
We should all be doing this.

00:49:30.041 --> 00:49:31.514
You've got experience-

00:49:31.514 --> 00:49:32.347
We should all have a timeline?

00:49:32.347 --> 00:49:33.870
We should all be writing down

00:49:33.870 --> 00:49:36.730
the things that happened to us.

00:49:36.730 --> 00:49:39.953
He had these memories
treasured in his mind.

00:49:40.890 --> 00:49:45.890
It was just the things
that, what happened to him,

00:49:46.920 --> 00:49:48.120
the way that he was drafted,

00:49:48.120 --> 00:49:49.557
and the near death experience,

00:49:49.557 --> 00:49:51.930
and I guess the pollen too,

00:49:51.930 --> 00:49:54.160
these were all things that were like,

00:49:54.160 --> 00:49:55.550
and the people that he met,

00:49:55.550 --> 00:49:57.570
and a lot of coaches
and basketball players

00:49:57.570 --> 00:49:59.580
that I did not go into those details here,

00:49:59.580 --> 00:50:01.370
were things that were
very important to him

00:50:01.370 --> 00:50:02.540
and were kind of extraordinary.

00:50:02.540 --> 00:50:04.310
Things that don't happen to a guy

00:50:04.310 --> 00:50:08.863
who just is a farmer and a
teacher in rural Harnett County.

00:50:09.790 --> 00:50:13.160
So he had this sense that
he lived a full life.

00:50:13.160 --> 00:50:14.640
And it brought a tremendous amount

00:50:14.640 --> 00:50:17.927
of comfort to all of us that read it.

00:50:17.927 --> 00:50:20.003
And his kids, to me, to mom.

00:50:21.270 --> 00:50:25.256
I read it to Christy and my
kids when they showed up.

00:50:25.256 --> 00:50:26.430
We went back through it.

00:50:26.430 --> 00:50:29.680
It was like, we were able to celebrate.

00:50:29.680 --> 00:50:33.780
It's like, wow, he really had a,

00:50:33.780 --> 00:50:37.943
he had a lot of treasures
in terms of memories.

00:50:39.280 --> 00:50:41.993
So this gotten you thinking,

00:50:43.724 --> 00:50:45.620
are you gonna start writing?

00:50:45.620 --> 00:50:49.900
Well for us, I'm like,
that's kind of like the,

00:50:49.900 --> 00:50:51.730
I don't know, the every man's application,

00:50:51.730 --> 00:50:54.600
but we're not every
man, we're weird people.

00:50:54.600 --> 00:50:59.600
We're weird people who have
this conversation on a podcast.

00:51:00.170 --> 00:51:03.050
And we've documented so
much of our lives here.

00:51:03.050 --> 00:51:04.590
It was the type of thing that, again,

00:51:04.590 --> 00:51:06.830
when you were, week before last,

00:51:06.830 --> 00:51:10.870
when you started talking
about your trip with Locke,

00:51:10.870 --> 00:51:13.530
it's like, you shared that with me

00:51:13.530 --> 00:51:16.370
and on this podcast with
everybody who's listening.

00:51:16.370 --> 00:51:17.790
You're also sharing it with people

00:51:17.790 --> 00:51:20.853
who would care to listen
after you're gone.

00:51:22.170 --> 00:51:23.367
There's so much stuff out there,

00:51:23.367 --> 00:51:25.450
and so many stories out there

00:51:25.450 --> 00:51:27.570
in Chia Lincoln and Good Mythical Morning,

00:51:27.570 --> 00:51:28.980
and especially this podcast

00:51:28.980 --> 00:51:31.380
when we're actively
